  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Sun May 10 2026\n\nMonday will be cooler with clearing skies after some morning cloud \ncover. Highs will be mostly in the upper 70s to lower 80s with north \nto northeast winds 7-14 mph. A surface high pressure ridge will \nshift southeast into the area Monday, and the surface ridge axis \nwill extend from the Ohio Valley southwest into central Texas Monday \nnight. Mostly clear skies and radiational cooling will allow \ntemperatures to drop into the lower to mid 50s for morning \nlows Tuesday.\n\nAn upper level ridge over the southwestern CONUS Monday will \ngradually shift east into Texas Tuesday into Wednesday, and across \nthe state Wednesday into Thursday. With associated subsidence, \nexpect generally clear skies and seasonably very warm temperatures.\nThe warmest temperatures each day will be in the northern and \nwestern Big Country and northwestern Concho Valley. Forecast highs \nare expected to range from the mid 80s southeast to lower 90s \nnorthwest on Tuesday, and from the mid/upper 80s southeast to \nmid/upper 90s northwest Wednesday and Thursday. A tighter pressure \ngradient will result in breezy south winds on Thursday. \n\nBy late week into the weekend, quasi-zonal flow aloft is progged \nover our area. Highs are expected to be a little hotter Friday and \nSaturday with a strengthened low-level thermal ridge edging into our \narea from the west. Also expecting breezy south winds to continue \nFriday and Saturday. Will have to watch for the possibility Saturday \nof a weak, embedded disturbance in the flow aloft to enter the area, \na dryline that may approach our western counties in the afternoon, \nand whether the trailing portion of a cold front can move south of \nthe Red River Saturday afternoon. While this is Day 7 with quite a \nbit of inherent uncertainty in the details, these features may have \nthe potential to bring a chance of showers and thunderstorms to \nportions of our area.",
